# BraX3: the vendor's health HAL without its wake alarm, added by 65-extra-init.
#
# android.hardware.health-service.mediatek (AIDL) keeps its battery chores on a
# CLOCK_BOOTTIME_ALARM timerfd (HealthLoop::WakeAlarmInit()): every 60 s on
# the charger and every 600 s on battery, and a BOOTTIME alarm wakes the SoC
# from suspend. On the Titan the same chore was 176 of 290 overnight wakeups.
# On the BraX3 the HAL held such a timerfd (clockid 9, 60 s interval) on 16 Nov 2026.
#
# Without CAP_WAKE_ALARM, timerfd_create() fails with EPERM; the HAL logs
# "wakealarm_init: timerfd_create failed" and carries on - the string is in
# this vendor binary. It still registers IHealth and reads the battery on every
# power_supply uevent and, while awake, on its own poll; it no longer wakes the
# phone for it.
#
# Same definition as the vendor's, plus override, minus WAKE_ALARM (BLOCK_SUSPEND
# stays: it only keeps the system up while the HAL handles a uevent). This file
# sorts after android.hardware.health-service.mediatek.rc, so init reads it last.
# vendor.charger (the same binary with --charger) only runs in charger mode and
# is left alone.
service vendor.health-mediatek /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.health-service.mediatek
    override
    class hal
    user system
    group system
    capabilities BLOCK_SUSPEND
    file /dev/kmsg w
